.md
# Lorentz Fitting of an Absorption Spectrum

This notebook builds a compact WLJS app for fitting absorption spectra with Lorentz oscillator line shapes. It imports measured data, initializes the resonance frequency, amplitude, and linewidth parameters, and exposes an interactive widget for tuning the model before reading out the fitted parameters and response function.

## Model and response functions

Each absorption feature is represented by the dissipative part of a damped Lorentz oscillator, $A \Gamma^2 \omega / [\Gamma^2 \omega^2 + (\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2]$. Here $\omega_0$ is the resonance frequency, $A$ controls oscillator strength, and $\Gamma$ (`width`) sets the linewidth.

:::warning
Multiple resonances are added linearly assuming that $\\varepsilon_{\\infty}$ is much larger than total contribution of the oscillators.
:::

The `sample` helper evaluates the summed response on a chosen frequency grid, with a constant background and scale/path factor used later by the fitting widget.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Lorentz Fitting of an Absorption Spectrum

This notebook builds a compact WLJS app for fitting absorption spectra with Lorentz oscillator line shapes. It imports measured data, initializes the resonance frequency, amplitude, and linewidth parameters, and exposes an interactive widget for tuning the model before reading out the fitted parameters and response function.

## Model and response functions

Each absorption feature is represented by the dissipative part of a damped Lorentz oscillator, $A \Gamma^2 \omega / [\Gamma^2 \omega^2 + (\omega_0^2 - \omega^2)^2]$. Here $\omega_0$ is the resonance frequency, $A$ controls oscillator strength, and $\Gamma$ (`width`) sets the linewidth.

:::warning
Multiple resonances are added linearly assuming that $\\varepsilon_{\\infty}$ is much larger than total contribution of the oscillators.
:::

The `sample` helper evaluates the summed response on a chosen frequency grid, with a constant background and scale/path factor used later by the fitting widget.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  InitGroup: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
lorentz[\[Omega]0_, A_, width_][\[Omega]_] :=  (*FB[*)((\[Omega])(*,*)/(*,*)(1))(*]FB*) (*FB[*)((A width \[Omega] width)(*,*)/(*,*)(((*SpB[*)Power[width(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*)) ((*SpB[*)Power[\[Omega](*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))+(*SpB[*)Power[(-((*SpB[*)Power[\[Omega](*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))+(*SpB[*)Power[\[Omega]0(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*))(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*)))(*]FB*);

sample[model_List, c_, L_, {min_, max_, step_}] := Module[{\[Omega]},
  Table[{\[Omega], L(\[Omega] c  + Sum[i[\[Omega]], {i,model}])}, {\[Omega], min, max, step}]
];

